-- goof.rofl — GOOF: Grothendieck Or Other Foundations.
--
-- One rule set. Nine books. The rules below do not name a single foundation,
-- and nothing in them changes when the foundation does: the switch is which
-- axioms a book holds, and the axioms live in that book's ledger.
--
-- WHY THIS DOMAIN AND NOT AN INVENTED LOGIC.
-- Euclidean and hyperbolic geometry are the same theorems derived from
-- different axiom sets. People fought over which set was true for two
-- thousand years, and the fight was decidable only because the axioms were
-- separable in exactly the sense a ledger is separable. Nothing here is
-- adapted to fit the engine; the corpus is Euclid's postulates, Saccheri's
-- programme, Legendre's two theorems and the standard neutral-geometry
-- development, with the historical attributions in README.md.
--
-- THE PERSPECTIVE IS A LEDGER, and here that is not an analogy.
-- docs/choosing-perspectives.md asks five questions before anything goes in
-- the slot. A foundation answers all five:
--
--   * two contradictory facts coexist legitimately under different books —
--     `thm[euclid](angle_sum_180)` and `thm[lobachevsky](defect_positive)`
--     are BOTH true, and neither is an error. That is the whole subject;
--   * a book is not computed from other facts. It is chosen;
--   * a book does not describe how a fact was obtained. `by_reductio` does,
--     and it is an attribute of a proof step, not a perspective;
--   * the writers are nameable and finite: the axiom-setter, via
--     `authority(euclid, euclid)`, writes the axioms; the deduction rules of
--     section 4 write the theorems. Two classes of writer, both declared —
--     the source and derived sub-kinds of ledger, in one book, which is what
--     a book of mathematics is;
--   * no literal ever needs two of them.
--
-- What would be WRONG here, and is worth naming because it is the obvious
-- first draft: `thm(euclid, angle_sum_180)` with the foundation as an
-- argument column. That is the mega-main anti-pattern. It loses `forged`
-- (anybody may write any foundation's axioms), it loses the audit of
-- cross-book reads, and it turns "the rules do not mention a foundation"
-- into a claim nobody can check, since the column is right there in every
-- rule.
--
-- THE COST THIS EXAMPLE PAID, AND GOT BACK. A rule polymorphic in the
-- ledger used to reduce to `$any` on both ends of the reflection, so
-- `boot.rofl` saw a flow from `$any` into `$any`, could not see that the
-- two were the SAME variable, and reported `leak[audit]($any, $any)` — a
-- false positive with no way to declare the bridge. examples/sus and
-- examples/aka met the same limit and could write one rule per ledger
-- instead; GOOF cannot, because one rule per ledger IS the thesis failing,
-- and that is what made the limit worth fixing rather than working around:
-- `src/reflect.ts` now records a variable perspective as itself, `$var(G)`,
-- and `boot.rofl` reads a flow whose two ends are one term as the identity
-- it is. The audit is empty here. `demo.ts` §1 still plants a REAL leak in
-- the same store, so the empty audit is not an alarm somebody switched off;
-- §3 still expands the rule set nine times over and checks that both
-- versions derive the same theorems, now measuring only the rule count.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. who may write in which book
-- =========================================================================
-- A ledger per foundation, and the writer is the person who fixed that
-- axiom set. Euclid gets two books because the Elements contains two
-- geometries — plane in I-VI, solid in XI-XIII — and the demo uses the
-- pair to change the dimension and nothing else.

authority(main,        goof).
authority(euclid,      euclid).
authority(solid,       euclid).
authority(saccheri,    saccheri).
authority(lobachevsky, lobachevsky).
authority(riemann,     riemann).
authority(schlafli,    schlafli).
authority(brouwer,     brouwer).
authority(frege,       frege).
authority(dacosta,     dacosta).

-- `proposition[main](P) :- axiom[G](P).` pools the axiom sets of all nine
-- books into one shared language, and `only_in[main](G, H, A)` reads a
-- second book to say what the first has that it lacks. [main] therefore
-- gathers from ledgers it does not name -- which is deliberate and is half
-- the thesis: nine axiom sets over ONE language, so that explosion has a
-- language to range over. `imports` cannot express it, because the source is
-- `$var("G")` and an import needs a registered perspective at both ends.
-- This is the sentence that can. It also licenses the walk
-- `$var("H") -> main -> $var("G")` -- one foundation's content reaching
-- another's -- which is a real crossing (README.md shows [frege] exploding
-- and taking four other books' axioms as theorems) and a declared one.
collects(main).

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. the nine books
-- =========================================================================
-- `foundation[G](Book, Year)` is the ledger declaring itself: the title the
-- axioms were published under and the year. It is also what BINDS the
-- ledger variable in the rules of section 4 — a rule that ranges over
-- foundations needs a positive premise in the ledger it ranges over, and
-- this is it. A book with no `foundation` entry is invisible to the whole
-- rule set, which is the correct reading of an unpublished axiom set.

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- the axioms, one book at a time. THIS IS THE ENTIRE SWITCH.
--
-- Read the blocks against each other: euclid and lobachevsky differ by one
-- line. That is the two-hundred-year argument, in the only place it is
-- allowed to live.
--
-- The `-- @who X` markers are not kernel syntax. They are comments that
-- examples/goof/demo.ts splits the file on, so that each book is loaded
-- under the identity of the person who published it, and `asserted_by` is
-- therefore the LOAD identity checked against `authority` — never a column
-- anybody could fill in with any name they liked. Section 11 of the demo
-- writes the hyperbolic axiom into Euclid's book and watches `forged`
-- surface it with no enforcement code anywhere.

-- SACCHERI, 1733 — neutral geometry: the four postulates, no parallel axiom
-- at all. Girolamo Saccheri assumed the fifth was a theorem of the other
-- four and spent thirty years looking for the contradiction that would
-- prove it. There is none, and section 5 of the demo is that search,
-- answered in a millisecond by `whynot`.
-- @who saccheri
foundation[saccheri](euclides_vindicatus, 1733).
axiom[saccheri](post1_two_points).
axiom[saccheri](post2_extend).
axiom[saccheri](post3_circle).
axiom[saccheri](post4_right_angles).
axiom[saccheri](archimedes).
axiom[saccheri](excluded_middle).
axiom[saccheri](ex_falso).
axiom[saccheri](dim_plane).

-- EUCLID, -300 — Saccheri's list plus Playfair's form of the fifth.
-- @who euclid
foundation[euclid](elements, -300).
axiom[euclid](post1_two_points).
axiom[euclid](post2_extend).
axiom[euclid](post3_circle).
axiom[euclid](post4_right_angles).
axiom[euclid](archimedes).
axiom[euclid](excluded_middle).
axiom[euclid](ex_falso).
axiom[euclid](dim_plane).
axiom[euclid](post5_unique).

-- LOBACHEVSKY, 1829 — the same list with one line changed. Bolyai reached
-- the same system independently in 1832; the demo credits both.
-- @who lobachevsky
foundation[lobachevsky](imaginary_geometry, 1829).
axiom[lobachevsky](post1_two_points).
axiom[lobachevsky](post2_extend).
axiom[lobachevsky](post3_circle).
axiom[lobachevsky](post4_right_angles).
axiom[lobachevsky](archimedes).
axiom[lobachevsky](excluded_middle).
axiom[lobachevsky](ex_falso).
axiom[lobachevsky](dim_plane).
axiom[lobachevsky](post5_many).

-- RIEMANN, 1854 — and here the tidy story about one switch breaks, which
-- is why the example keeps it. Elliptic geometry cannot merely deny the
-- fifth: a line on a sphere is unbounded but FINITE, so postulate 2 goes
-- too. Two lines changed, not one, and section 6 measures which theorems
-- die of which change. The neutral theorems that lean on post2 — Euclid
-- I.16 above all — are lost here and nowhere else.
-- @who riemann
foundation[riemann](habilitation, 1854).
axiom[riemann](post1_two_points).
axiom[riemann](post2_finite).
axiom[riemann](post3_circle).
axiom[riemann](post4_right_angles).
axiom[riemann](archimedes).
axiom[riemann](excluded_middle).
axiom[riemann](ex_falso).
axiom[riemann](dim_plane).
axiom[riemann](post5_none).

-- SOLID / SCHLAFLI — Euclid's plane list with the dimension axiom changed
-- and nothing else. Ludwig Schlafli wrote n-dimensional geometry in 1852
-- and could not get it published. The pair exists for one derived fact:
-- a knot is knotted in three dimensions and unties in four.
-- @who euclid
foundation[solid](elements_xi, -300).
axiom[solid](post1_two_points).
axiom[solid](post2_extend).
axiom[solid](post3_circle).
axiom[solid](post4_right_angles).
axiom[solid](archimedes).
axiom[solid](excluded_middle).
axiom[solid](ex_falso).
axiom[solid](post5_unique).
axiom[solid](dim_space).

-- @who schlafli
foundation[schlafli](vielfache_kontinuitaet, 1852).
axiom[schlafli](post1_two_points).
axiom[schlafli](post2_extend).
axiom[schlafli](post3_circle).
axiom[schlafli](post4_right_angles).
axiom[schlafli](archimedes).
axiom[schlafli](excluded_middle).
axiom[schlafli](ex_falso).
axiom[schlafli](post5_unique).
axiom[schlafli](dim_four).

-- BROUWER, 1908 — Euclid's geometry, intuitionist logic. One line removed:
-- excluded middle. The geometry is untouched, and the theorems that stop
-- being derivable are exactly those whose only proof is a reductio.
-- @who brouwer
foundation[brouwer](unreliability, 1908).
axiom[brouwer](post1_two_points).
axiom[brouwer](post2_extend).
axiom[brouwer](post3_circle).
axiom[brouwer](post4_right_angles).
axiom[brouwer](archimedes).
axiom[brouwer](ex_falso).
axiom[brouwer](dim_plane).
axiom[brouwer](post5_unique).

-- FREGE, 1893 — the book that really did explode. The Grundgesetze was
-- consistent-looking until Russell's letter of 1902, and classical logic
-- turns one contradiction into every sentence. Here the contradiction is
-- put in by hand — both parallel axioms at once — and `ex_falso` does the
-- rest. Nothing about the geometry causes it.
-- @who frege
foundation[frege](grundgesetze, 1893).
axiom[frege](post1_two_points).
axiom[frege](post2_extend).
axiom[frege](post3_circle).
axiom[frege](post4_right_angles).
axiom[frege](archimedes).
axiom[frege](excluded_middle).
axiom[frege](ex_falso).
axiom[frege](dim_plane).
axiom[frege](post5_unique).
axiom[frege](post5_many).

-- DA COSTA, 1963 — the same contradiction, and one line fewer: no
-- `ex_falso`. Newton da Costa's C-systems keep a contradiction local
-- instead of letting it decide everything. The two books differ by that
-- single axiom, and section 9 counts the difference in theorems.
-- @who dacosta
foundation[dacosta](calculs_inconsistants, 1963).
axiom[dacosta](post1_two_points).
axiom[dacosta](post2_extend).
axiom[dacosta](post3_circle).
axiom[dacosta](post4_right_angles).
axiom[dacosta](archimedes).
axiom[dacosta](excluded_middle).
axiom[dacosta](dim_plane).
axiom[dacosta](post5_unique).
axiom[dacosta](post5_many).

-- @who goof
-- Which propositions deny each other. `contrary` is stated once per pair and
-- symmetrised by a rule; it carries no logical force by itself, and a book
-- holding both members of a pair is not an error — see `clash` in §4 and
-- what the two books above do with it. Axioms and derived theorems are both
-- here, so a book can be caught contradicting itself several steps away from
-- its axioms and not only in them.
contrary(post5_unique, post5_many).
contrary(post5_unique, post5_none).
contrary(post5_many, post5_none).
contrary(post2_extend, post2_finite).
contrary(dim_plane, dim_space).
contrary(dim_plane, dim_four).
contrary(dim_space, dim_four).
contrary(angle_sum_180, defect_positive).
contrary(angle_sum_180, excess_positive).
contrary(defect_positive, excess_positive).
contrary(rectangle_exists, no_rectangle).
contrary(similar_triangles_exist, aaa_congruence).
contrary(knots_exist, no_knots_at_all).
contrary(knots_exist, every_knot_unties).

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. the corpus: proof steps, shared by every book
-- =========================================================================
-- `step(S, P)` concludes P; `needs(S, K, Q)` is its K-th premise, 1-based;
-- `need_count(S, N)` says how many. The indexing is moot.rofl's device and
-- it is here for the same reason: "every premise of S holds" is a universal
-- over an open set, the obvious spelling puts the rule in a negative cycle
-- with itself, and walking the premises by index turns it into a positive
-- recursion the engine stratifies without complaint.
--
-- A premise may be an axiom or another proposition; nothing distinguishes
-- them, because `thm[G](A) :- axiom[G](A)` makes an axiom a theorem with an
-- empty proof. That is also why a LOGICAL principle is written as an
-- ordinary premise: a step that argues by contradiction names
-- `excluded_middle` among its premises, and a book that does not hold it
-- loses the step. No special case anywhere for logic.

-- ---- the neutral core: no parallel axiom is named by any of these -------

-- I.4 side-angle-side, and I.5 pons asinorum. The two theorems that survive
-- every foundation in this file, including the exploding one.
step(s_sas, sas_congruence).
needs(s_sas, 1, post1_two_points).
needs(s_sas, 2, post4_right_angles).
need_count(s_sas, 2).

step(s_pons, pons_asinorum).
needs(s_pons, 1, post1_two_points).
needs(s_pons, 2, post4_right_angles).
need_count(s_pons, 2).

step(s_tri_ineq, triangle_inequality).
needs(s_tri_ineq, 1, post1_two_points).
needs(s_tri_ineq, 2, sas_congruence).
need_count(s_tri_ineq, 2).

-- I.16, the exterior angle theorem: an exterior angle exceeds either remote
-- interior angle. The proof doubles a median and needs the doubled segment
-- to exist, so it needs post2. On a sphere it is FALSE, and everything
-- below it in this corpus falls with it. This one step is why elliptic
-- geometry is not "Euclid minus the fifth".
step(s_exterior, exterior_angle).
needs(s_exterior, 1, post1_two_points).
needs(s_exterior, 2, post2_extend).
needs(s_exterior, 3, post4_right_angles).
need_count(s_exterior, 3).

-- I.27: alternate angles equal implies the lines never meet. Neutral.
step(s_i27, alternate_angles_parallel).
needs(s_i27, 1, exterior_angle).
need_count(s_i27, 1).

-- I.31: through a point off a line there is AT LEAST one parallel. Neutral,
-- and the fifth postulate is only about how many. Two millennia of readers
-- have taken existence and uniqueness for one statement; here they are two
-- facts with different supports, and section 4 counts the second.
step(s_i31, parallel_exists).
needs(s_i31, 1, alternate_angles_parallel).
needs(s_i31, 2, post3_circle).
need_count(s_i31, 2).

-- Saccheri-Legendre: the angle sum of a triangle is at most 180 degrees.
-- Neutral, and the first step in the corpus that argues by contradiction:
-- assume an excess, halve the triangle repeatedly, and the excess
-- accumulates past two right angles. Needs Archimedes for the halving to
-- terminate and excluded middle for the reductio.
step(s_sacc_leg, angle_sum_at_most_180).
needs(s_sacc_leg, 1, exterior_angle).
needs(s_sacc_leg, 2, archimedes).
needs(s_sacc_leg, 3, excluded_middle).
need_count(s_sacc_leg, 3).

-- The defect of a triangle is additive under subdivision by a cevian. This
-- is the theorem section 7 turns into arithmetic; it is neutral, and it is
-- what makes an angle sum a function of area rather than of shape.
step(s_defect_add, defect_additive).
needs(s_defect_add, 1, post1_two_points).
need_count(s_defect_add, 1).

-- Legendre's second theorem: if ONE triangle has angle sum 180, every
-- triangle does. Also a reductio.
step(s_legendre2, all_or_none).
needs(s_legendre2, 1, angle_sum_at_most_180).
needs(s_legendre2, 2, defect_additive).
needs(s_legendre2, 3, excluded_middle).
need_count(s_legendre2, 3).

-- ---- the euclidean branch ----------------------------------------------

step(s_playfair, angle_sum_180).
needs(s_playfair, 1, post5_unique).
needs(s_playfair, 2, alternate_angles_parallel).
need_count(s_playfair, 2).

step(s_pythagoras, pythagoras).
needs(s_pythagoras, 1, angle_sum_180).
needs(s_pythagoras, 2, sas_congruence).
need_count(s_pythagoras, 2).

step(s_thales, thales_semicircle).
needs(s_thales, 1, angle_sum_180).
needs(s_thales, 2, pons_asinorum).
need_count(s_thales, 2).

step(s_rectangle, rectangle_exists).
needs(s_rectangle, 1, angle_sum_180).
need_count(s_rectangle, 1).

step(s_similar, similar_triangles_exist).
needs(s_similar, 1, angle_sum_180).
needs(s_similar, 2, post3_circle).
need_count(s_similar, 2).

-- ---- the hyperbolic branch ---------------------------------------------

step(s_defect_pos, defect_positive).
needs(s_defect_pos, 1, post5_many).
needs(s_defect_pos, 2, angle_sum_at_most_180).
needs(s_defect_pos, 3, all_or_none).
need_count(s_defect_pos, 3).

step(s_defect_prop, defect_proportional_to_area).
needs(s_defect_prop, 1, defect_positive).
needs(s_defect_prop, 2, defect_additive).
needs(s_defect_prop, 3, archimedes).
need_count(s_defect_prop, 3).

step(s_no_rect, no_rectangle).
needs(s_no_rect, 1, defect_positive).
need_count(s_no_rect, 1).

-- ---- the elliptic branch -----------------------------------------------
-- The excess is positive, and the proof does NOT go through I.16: on a
-- sphere the exterior angle theorem is false, so the whole neutral tower
-- above is unavailable and the excess is established from the finiteness of
-- lines directly. Which is exactly why riemann's provenance polynomial in
-- section 6 has a different shape from lobachevsky's.
step(s_excess_pos, excess_positive).
needs(s_excess_pos, 1, post5_none).
needs(s_excess_pos, 2, post2_finite).
needs(s_excess_pos, 3, defect_additive).
need_count(s_excess_pos, 3).

step(s_excess_prop, excess_proportional_to_area).
needs(s_excess_prop, 1, excess_positive).
needs(s_excess_prop, 2, defect_additive).
needs(s_excess_prop, 3, archimedes).
need_count(s_excess_prop, 3).

-- Angle-angle-angle congruence: equal angles force equal size, so there is
-- no similarity. TWO steps conclude it, one per non-euclidean branch, and
-- no book holds both. A proposition with several proofs is ordinary; the
-- counting semiring is what tells them apart.
step(s_aaa_hyp, aaa_congruence).
needs(s_aaa_hyp, 1, defect_proportional_to_area).
need_count(s_aaa_hyp, 1).

step(s_aaa_ell, aaa_congruence).
needs(s_aaa_ell, 1, excess_proportional_to_area).
need_count(s_aaa_ell, 1).

-- ---- dimension ---------------------------------------------------------
-- The one line the spec asks for from the dimension axis, because it is
-- counter-intuitive and it is derived rather than declared: a closed curve
-- cannot be knotted in the plane, is knotted in space, and unties in four
-- dimensions (Wu 1958, Zeeman 1960 — an isotopy exists in codimension 3).
-- "Obvious" is a property of the model you grew up in.
step(s_flat_curve, no_knots_at_all).
needs(s_flat_curve, 1, dim_plane).
need_count(s_flat_curve, 1).

step(s_knots, knots_exist).
needs(s_knots, 1, dim_space).
need_count(s_knots, 1).

step(s_unknotting, every_knot_unties).
needs(s_unknotting, 1, dim_four).
need_count(s_unknotting, 1).

-- Which steps argue by contradiction. Reporting only: the logical principle
-- is already a premise, so nothing derives from this table. It exists so a
-- reader can ask "which of my theorems needed a reductio" without reading
-- every proof, and section 8 asks it.
by_reductio(s_sacc_leg).
by_reductio(s_legendre2).

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. the figure: one triangle, cut twice
-- =========================================================================
-- Areas are data — this is a figure someone drew, not a theorem — and they
-- are additive by construction: 2 + 3 = 5, 5 + 7 = 12. What is NOT data is
-- the angle sum. Section 7 derives it two ways for every compound triangle,
-- once from the area law and once from the parts, and the two agree only
-- because the defect is additive and proportional. In a book where they
-- disagree, `disagree` says so instead of picking a winner.

leaf(t_a, 2).
leaf(t_b, 3).
leaf(t_left, 5).
leaf(t_right, 7).
leaf(t_whole, 12).

cut(t_left, t_a, t_b).
cut(t_whole, t_left, t_right).

-- Two triangles of different size, offered to each book as candidates for
-- similarity. Whether they CAN have the same angles is derived, not given.
pair(t_left, t_whole).

-- One knot, so the dimension steps have something to be about.
knot(trefoil).

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. THE ONE RULE SET
-- =========================================================================
-- Everything below this marker is rules, and not one of them names a
-- foundation. `[G]` is a ledger variable: the same rule reads the book it
-- writes into, whichever book that is. demo.ts §3 splits the file here,
-- substitutes a book name for `[G]` nine times over, loads the result and
-- checks that the expanded program derives exactly the same facts — the
-- price of naming the ledger, counted in rules.
--
-- Every head that is not `[G]` names its perspective explicitly, `[main]`
-- included. That USED TO BE the fix for a false alarm: a head with no
-- perspective defaulted to `[main]` without setting the explicit flag, the
-- kernel emitted no `bridge_decl` for it, and `leak[audit]` fired on a rule
-- doing nothing wrong. The mechanism is gone — `crossing` no longer reads
-- `bridge_decl`, and nothing emits it — so the bracket now decides nothing in
-- either direction. It is kept because naming the ledger you write is worth
-- doing on its own, not because it silences anything.
--
-- ONE ROW IS LEFT REPORTING HERE, AND IT IS A GAP IN THE LANGUAGE.
-- `leak[audit](main, $var("G"))`: a rule reads [main] — the arithmetic and
-- structural helpers nobody gave a book to — and writes the ledger VARIABLE.
-- The crossing's DESTINATION is unnameable, and every instrument this
-- language has points the other way. `imports(To, From)` needs both ends
-- registered; `collects(X)` says "X gathers from books it does not name",
-- which is the mirror image and does not fit.
--
-- The line that silences it, `imports($var("G"), main).`, is writable —
-- `$var` is typeable since `$` became a leading name character — and it is
-- WRONG: it licenses by the SPELLING OF THE VARIABLE, so renaming `?G` to
-- `?H` throughout would revoke a licence that nothing about the program
-- changed. examples/npc carries the same row for the same reason
-- (`world -> $var("A")`). Two demos, one missing sentence. An open gap beats
-- a closed one in the wrong instrument, so both rows stand and are named.
--
-- @rules

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.1 derivation: an axiom is a theorem, and a step whose premises are all
-- theorems concludes one.

thm[G](P) :- axiom[G](P).

-- "every premise of S holds in G", walked by index. The base case is the
-- position past the last premise, and it needs `foundation[G]` to bind the
-- ledger: without it the rule would be true of every book that does not
-- exist. The recursive case reads premise K, demands it, and moves on.
holds_from[G](S, K1) :- foundation[G](_, _), need_count(S, N), K1 is N + 1.
holds_from[G](S, K)  :- needs(S, K, P), thm[G](P), K1 is K + 1, holds_from[G](S, K1).

thm[G](P) :- step(S, P), holds_from[G](S, 1).

-- The vocabulary of the whole inquiry: everything any book talks about.
-- Explicitly `[main]`, which is what keeps it out of `leak`.
proposition[main](P) :- step(_, P).
proposition[main](P) :- axiom[G](P).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.2 contradiction, and what a book does with one.
--
-- `clash` records that a book holds two theorems that deny each other. It
-- is a fact, not an error: nothing in the engine reacts to it. What reacts
-- is the next rule, and only in a book that holds `ex_falso`.
--
-- This is the paraconsistency demonstration and it is worth being exact
-- about what it does and does not show. ROFL is not paraconsistent by
-- accident of having no negation-as-failure at the object level — it simply
-- has no ex falso rule of its own. So explosion is written HERE, as an
-- ordinary rule licensed by an ordinary axiom, and the difference between
-- Frege's book and da Costa's is that axiom and nothing else.

opposes[main](A, B) :- contrary(A, B).
opposes[main](B, A) :- contrary(A, B).

clash[G](A, B) :- thm[G](A), thm[G](B), opposes[main](A, B).

-- `explodes` exists as its own relation rather than being folded into the
-- rule below it, and the reason is `whynot`. Every failed proof in every
-- book has to consider the possibility that the book explodes, because that
-- is genuinely one of the three ways to derive anything here. Given a name,
-- that branch reads as one line — "the book does not explode" — instead of
-- an enumeration of every pair of theorems that failed to contradict each
-- other. The demonstration's shape is part of the model's design.
explodes[G](P) :- clash[G](_, _), axiom[G](ex_falso), proposition[main](P).

thm[G](P) :- explodes[G](P).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.3 how many parallels pass through the point.
--
-- The count is not asserted anywhere. Existence is the neutral theorem
-- I.31; the parallel axiom decides how many witnesses that existence has,
-- and the last rule is the hyperbolic construction: between any two
-- distinct parallels lies another. In euclid it never fires, because there
-- is only one parallel and it needs two DIFFERENT ones. In riemann there
-- are none to begin with — and note that the elliptic book fails to derive
-- even `parallel_exists`, because I.31 rests on I.16 which rests on post2.
--
-- The counting semiring folded over the support of these four rules answers
-- "how many": 1 for euclid, 0 for riemann, and INFINITE for lobachevsky,
-- because `interior` is on a cycle of the support graph and the CLOSED
-- discipline closes it. See README.md for what that count is a count OF —
-- derivations, which coincide with lines here only because each application
-- of the construction exhibits a new line.

parallel[G](sole)     :- thm[G](parallel_exists), axiom[G](post5_unique).
parallel[G](limit_a)  :- thm[G](parallel_exists), axiom[G](post5_many).
parallel[G](limit_b)  :- thm[G](parallel_exists), axiom[G](post5_many).
parallel[G](interior) :- parallel[G](X), parallel[G](Y), X != Y.

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.4 the angle sum as a function of area.
--
-- `law[G](K)` is the defect coefficient of the book, and it is DERIVED from
-- which theorem about the angle sum that book proved: flat, hyperbolic,
-- elliptic. A book that proved none of the three — neutral geometry — has
-- no law, and therefore no angle sum at all, which is the correct answer
-- and the one Saccheri would have wanted.
--
-- Then two rules for the sum itself. The leaf rule applies the law to an
-- area. The cut rule composes a triangle from its parts and uses no law at
-- all: the two angles at the foot of a cevian make a straight line, so the
-- sums of the parts overcount by exactly 180. The cut rule is NEUTRAL — it
-- is the additivity of the defect, spelled in degrees — and every compound
-- triangle therefore gets its sum derived twice, by routes that share no
-- rule. They agree, and `disagree` is the gate that would say so if they
-- did not.

law[G](0)  :- thm[G](angle_sum_180).
law[G](1)  :- thm[G](defect_proportional_to_area).
law[G](-1) :- thm[G](excess_proportional_to_area).

angle_sum[G](T, S) :- leaf(T, A), law[G](K), S is 180 - K * A.
angle_sum[G](T, S) :- cut(T, T1, T2), angle_sum[G](T1, S1), angle_sum[G](T2, S2),
                      S is S1 + S2 - 180.

disagree[G](T, S1, S2) :- angle_sum[G](T, S1), angle_sum[G](T, S2), S1 < S2.

-- Similarity, derived rather than declared. Two triangles of different area
-- can share their angles only if the angle sum does not depend on the area.
-- Where it does, the smaller triangle has a bigger sum, the two cannot be
-- equiangular, and `aaa_congruence` above is the same statement proved
-- symbolically — two routes to "a triangle knows its size", one through the
-- corpus and one through arithmetic.

similar_possible[G](T1, T2) :- pair(T1, T2), angle_sum[G](T1, S), angle_sum[G](T2, S).
size_shows[G](T1, T2, D)    :- pair(T1, T2), angle_sum[G](T1, S1), angle_sum[G](T2, S2),
                               S1 != S2, D is S1 - S2.

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.5 knots. The three steps of §2 do the work; this rule only reads their
-- conclusions back onto the object, so that the answer names the knot.

knot_state[G](K, unknottable) :- knot(K), thm[G](no_knots_at_all).
knot_state[G](K, knotted)     :- knot(K), thm[G](knots_exist).
knot_state[G](K, unties)      :- knot(K), thm[G](every_knot_unties).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.6 reporting: which theorems needed a reductio, and which axioms a book
-- has that another does not. Both are ordinary derived relations in
-- `[main]`, and both exist so that a person can ask the question without
-- reading nine axiom blocks by eye.

reductio_needed[main](P, S) :- step(S, P), by_reductio(S).

-- `only_in(G, H, A)` — A is an axiom of G that H does not hold. The pair of
-- books is bound by two `foundation` premises, so the relation is over
-- books that exist; the negated premise is what makes it a difference.
-- This is the measurement behind "one line changed".
only_in[main](G, H, A) :- axiom[G](A), foundation[H](_, _), not axiom[H](A).

-- Acceptance scenario (examples/goof/demo.ts runs all of it):
--   ? unstratified(X)                  -> empty
--   ? leak[audit](A, B)                -> empty (planted leak: one row)
--   ? thm[euclid](angle_sum_180)       -> holds;  thm[lobachevsky] -> does not
--   whynot thm[saccheri](angle_sum_180)-> names post5_unique, twice over
--   counting parallel[G](interior)     -> 1 / 0 / infinitely many
--   provenance thm[euclid](pythagoras) -> every monomial contains post5_unique
--   ? disagree[dacosta](T, A, B)       -> non-empty, and explodes[dacosta] empty
--   ? knot_state[schlafli](trefoil, S) -> unties
